//! Where ferrotape reads otherwise than the reference readers.
//!
//! ferrotape reads an archive as GNU tar, bsdtar, Go's `archive/tar` and
//! Python's `tarfile` read it, and refuses one they read differently. This
//! page lists every kind of archive on which ferrotape reads otherwise than
//! at least one of them:
//!
//! - Where they disagree among themselves
//! - Where ferrotape refuses an archive one of them reads
//! - Where ferrotape reads an archive one of them refuses
//!
//! Each was measured with bsdtar 3.8.11, Go 1.27.0, and GNU tar 1.35 and
//! Python 3.12.3 as Ubuntu 24.04 packages them. The two Ubuntu builds include
//! fixes their own releases lack.
//!
//! Each row names the cases of the repository's catalog (`catalog/`) that
//! show it. A case holds the archive, each reader's recorded reading, and
//! ferrotape's verdict in every setting. The catalog does not record
//! extraction, an attribute's value, or the mode GNU tar or bsdtar lists. A
//! row naming no case was measured by one of those. "Strict" and
//! "Compatible" are the two levels ([`Level`](crate::Level)). Where a row
//! names neither, both read alike.
//!
//! Each reference reader has a column. Go's `archive/tar` extracts nothing,
//! so its column gives what it reports. Some Rust readers are counted at the
//! Strict level alone, and ferrotape's column names them where they read
//! otherwise. Where only they disagree, Compatible reads as the reference
//! readers do.
//!
//! # Where an archive begins and ends
//!
//! | Archive | GNU tar | bsdtar | Python | Go | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A stream that ends before its first header | not an archive | an empty archive | not an archive | an empty archive | not a tar archive ([`NotATar`](crate::ErrorKind::NotATar)) | `framing/an-empty-stream` |
//! | A stream that ends after a pax header, before its member | an empty archive | fails | fails | an empty archive | refused ([`Truncated`](crate::ErrorKind::Truncated)) | `framing/a-stream-that-ends-after-metadata` |
//! | A stream that ends inside a member's data | lists the member, then fails | lists the member, then fails | fails before listing it | lists the member, then fails | the member, then refused when its data is read | `framing/a-stream-that-ends-inside-data` |
//! | A zero block, then a header | ends the archive at the zero block. With `--ignore-zeros`, reads on | ends the archive at the zero block | ends the archive at the zero block | fails at the header | Strict: refused ([`TrailingData`](crate::ErrorKind::TrailingData)). Compatible: the archive ends at the zero block | `framing/one-zero-block-then-a-member` |
//! | Anything but zeros after the end marker | stops at the marker. With `--ignore-zeros`, reads on | stops at the marker | stops at the marker | stops at the marker | never read as archive. Strict: refused by [`Reader::finish`](crate::Reader::finish) | `framing/a-member-after-the-end-marker` |
//!
//! # Header fields
//!
//! | Archive | GNU tar | bsdtar | Python | Go | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A device number that does not parse, in any header with magic | reads a regular file's, and a device's with a wrong number | fails | fails | fails | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-regular-file-with-an-unreadable-device-number`, `headers/a-character-device-with-an-unreadable-device-number` |
//! | A device number that does not parse, in a v7 header or one under any other magic | reads on | fails at the first header, and reads on after it | reads every header's device fields. Ends the archive at one it cannot read, having listed the members before it, or refuses it where it is the first header | reads on | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-v7-header-with-an-unreadable-device-number`, `headers/a-v7-header-with-an-unreadable-device-number-first` |
//! | A numeric field holding digits, then a space and more, in its mode, ids, size, time or device numbers | reads the first digits | reads the first digits where the header is not the archive's first, and fails at the first | refuses the archive where it is the first header. Otherwise ends the archive there, having listed the members before it | fails at the header | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-size-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits`, `headers/a-mode-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits-after-a-member`, `headers/a-device-number-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits-after-a-member` |
//! | A numeric field beginning with a NUL and holding anything but NULs after it, in its mode, ids, size or time, or a device's numbers | reads the digits after the NUL | reads zero | reads zero | reads the digits after the NUL | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)). A field of NULs alone is zero, as every reader reads it. Another member's device numbers are read as zero, since GNU tar reads them only for a device | `headers/a-size-field-beginning-with-a-nul`, `headers/a-mode-field-beginning-with-a-nul-after-a-member` |
//! | A numeric field of spaces alone, in its mode, ids, size or time, or a device's numbers | reports blanks where a number belongs, and reads all bits set: a mode of `7777`, a device number of 4294967295 | reads zero | reads zero | reads zero | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)). A field of spaces ending in a NUL is zero, as every reader reads it | `headers/a-mode-field-of-spaces`, `headers/a-device-number-of-spaces-on-a-character-device` |
//! | A checksum written in GNU's base-256 form | passes over the header as damaged, and reads the next block as a header | passes over the header as damaged, and reads the next block as a header. Does not recognize an archive whose first header it is | reads the header | fails at the header | refused ([`Checksum`](crate::ErrorKind::Checksum)) | `headers/a-checksum-in-base-256-after-a-member` |
//! | A checksum field beginning with a NUL, the right sum after it | reads the header | does not recognize an archive whose first header it is, and passes over it as damaged after that | refuses an archive whose first header it is, and ends the archive at it after that | reads the header | refused ([`Checksum`](crate::ErrorKind::Checksum)) | `headers/a-checksum-beginning-with-a-nul` |
//! | A GNU header that is not typeflag `S` holding old GNU sparse slots (bytes 386 to 481), or, for a member that has data, a real size (483 to 494) | reads the header's own fields | reads a regular file's real size as its size, and fails on a slot | reads the header's own fields | reads the header's own fields | refused ([`SparseFields`](crate::ErrorKind::SparseFields)). The extension flag alone, and a real size on a member that has no data, are read as every reader reads them | `headers/a-gnu-regular-file-with-an-old-sparse-real-size`, `headers/a-gnu-regular-file-with-old-sparse-slots`, `headers/a-gnu-directory-with-old-sparse-slots` |
//! | A size of 2<sup>60</sup> or more, declared by a header or a pax `size` record | reads a member that has no data on past it, and a regular file until its data runs out | stops at the member ("Tar entry size overflow"), listing only the members before it | reads a member that has no data on past it, and fails at once on a regular file | reads a member that has no data on past it, and a regular file until its data runs out | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField), [`PaxValue`](crate::ErrorKind::PaxValue)). A size of 2<sup>60</sup> - 1 is read as any other | `headers/a-size-of-2-to-the-60-on-a-symbolic-link`, `headers/a-size-just-under-2-to-the-60-on-a-symbolic-link`, `pax/a-size-record-of-2-to-the-60` |
//! | A GNU or v7 header whose bytes at offset 345 begin with one that is not NUL, as in a GNU incremental dump | names the member from its name field | names the member from its name field | joins those bytes to the name as a prefix | names the member from its name field | refused ([`PrefixArea`](crate::ErrorKind::PrefixArea)) | `headers/bytes-in-a-gnu-header-s-prefix-area`, `headers/bytes-in-a-v7-header-s-prefix-area` |
//! | A POSIX header that names its member with a prefix ending in `/` | joins the prefix and the name with a `/` (`a//b`) | adds no `/` (`a/b`) | joins the prefix and the name with a `/` (`a//b`) | joins the prefix and the name with a `/` (`a//b`) | refused ([`PrefixSlash`](crate::ErrorKind::PrefixSlash)). Read where a pax `path` or a long name replaces the name |  |
//! | A POSIX header ending in star's trailer, `tar` and a NUL in its last four bytes, that names its member with a prefix longer than star's 131 bytes | joins the whole prefix to the name | joins the whole prefix to the name | joins the whole prefix to the name | reads the header in star's format: the prefix's first 131 bytes, then star's times, failing where they are not numbers | refused ([`StarPrefix`](crate::ErrorKind::StarPrefix)). Read where a pax `path` or a long name replaces the name | `headers/a-prefix-past-star-s-131-bytes-under-its-trailer` |
